"use strict";(globalThis.webpackChunkcasdoor_website=globalThis.webpackChunkcasdoor_website||[]).push([[8316],{96484(e){e.exports=JSON.parse('{"archive":{"blogPosts":[{"id":"casdoor-on-kubernetes","metadata":{"permalink":"/de/blog/casdoor-on-kubernetes","editUrl":"https://github.com/casdoor/casdoor-website/edit/master/i18n/de/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/2026-10-07-casdoor-on-kubernetes.mdx","source":"@site/i18n/de/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/2026-10-07-casdoor-on-kubernetes.mdx","title":"Running Casdoor on Kubernetes: Official Helm Chart and Configuration as Code","description":"Casdoor has an official Helm chart. It lives in the main repository under manifests/casdoor, is published with every Casdoor release to GHCR, and is listed on Artifact Hub. Starting with Casdoor v4.18.0, the chart can also hold your organizations, applications, users and providers as declarative configuration: change them in the values file, run helm upgrade, and the running pods apply the change without a restart.","date":"2026-10-07T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"Casdoor","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/casdoor"},{"inline":true,"label":"Kubernetes","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/kubernetes"},{"inline":true,"label":"Helm","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/helm"},{"inline":true,"label":"GitOps","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/git-ops"},{"inline":true,"label":"Configuration as Code","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/configuration-as-code"},{"inline":true,"label":"IAM","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/iam"},{"inline":true,"label":"SSO","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/sso"}],"readingTime":5.45,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Yang Luo","title":"Casdoor Maintainer","url":"https://github.com/hsluoyz","imageURL":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/hsluoyz","key":"hsluoyz","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"casdoor-on-kubernetes","title":"Running Casdoor on Kubernetes: Official Helm Chart and Configuration as Code","authors":["hsluoyz"],"tags":["Casdoor","Kubernetes","Helm","GitOps","Configuration as Code","IAM","SSO"],"date":"2026-10-07T00:00:00.000Z"},"unlisted":false,"nextItem":{"title":"Casdoor Joins CNCF Cloud Native Landscape","permalink":"/de/blog/casdoor-joins-cncf-landscape"}},"content":"Casdoor has an official Helm chart. It lives in the main repository under [`manifests/casdoor`](https://github.com/casdoor/casdoor/tree/master/manifests/casdoor), is published with every Casdoor release to GHCR, and is listed on [Artifact Hub](https://artifacthub.io/packages/helm/casdoor/casdoor). Starting with Casdoor v4.18.0, the chart can also hold your organizations, applications, users and providers as declarative configuration: change them in the values file, run `helm upgrade`, and the running pods apply the change without a restart.\\n\\nThis post walks through a Kubernetes deployment from the first `helm install` to a setup you can keep in Git.\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n## Install\\n\\nYou need Helm v3.8+ and a cluster:\\n\\n```shell\\nhelm install casdoor oci://ghcr.io/casdoor/helm-charts/casdoor\\n```\\n\\nThe chart version is the Casdoor version, so `--version 4.18.0` installs Casdoor 4.18.0. Without an Ingress or Gateway, the service is only reachable inside the cluster. To try it, forward a port:\\n\\n```shell\\nkubectl port-forward svc/casdoor 8000:8000\\n```\\n\\nThen open `http://localhost:8000` and sign in with `admin` / `123`.\\n\\n## A production setup\\n\\nThe defaults are meant for a quick try: Casdoor stores its data in SQLite inside the container, so it is lost when the pod is replaced. For a real deployment, put the settings below in a `values.yaml` file and install with `helm install casdoor oci://ghcr.io/casdoor/helm-charts/casdoor -f values.yaml`.\\n\\n### External database\\n\\n```yaml\\ndatabase:\\n  driver: postgres # or mysql, cockroachdb\\n  host: postgres.db.svc.cluster.local\\n  user: casdoor\\n  password: change-me\\n  databaseName: casdoor\\n  sslMode: require\\n```\\n\\nTo keep the database password out of the values file, put the whole `app.conf` in a Secret and set `configFromSecret` to its name.\\n\\n### Exposing Casdoor\\n\\nThe chart supports both a classic Ingress and the Gateway API. With an Ingress controller and cert-manager:\\n\\n```yaml\\ningress:\\n  enabled: true\\n  className: nginx\\n  annotations:\\n    cert-manager.io/cluster-issuer: letsencrypt-prod\\n  hosts:\\n    - host: door.example.com\\n      paths:\\n        - path: /\\n          pathType: Prefix\\n  tls:\\n    - secretName: casdoor-tls\\n      hosts:\\n        - door.example.com\\n```\\n\\nWith the Gateway API (Istio, Envoy Gateway, Cilium, Kong, NGINX Gateway Fabric and others), attach an `HTTPRoute` to an existing Gateway:\\n\\n```yaml\\ngatewayApi:\\n  enabled: true\\n  parentRefs:\\n    - name: my-gateway\\n      namespace: gateway-system\\n  hostnames:\\n    - door.example.com\\n```\\n\\nThe chart can also create the Gateway itself and add an HTTP to HTTPS redirect, see the [Helm guide](/docs/basic/try-with-helm#exposing-casdoor).\\n\\n### More than one replica\\n\\nCasdoor keeps sign-in sessions on local disk by default. Before running more than one replica, point all of them to the same Redis, so a user stays signed in whichever pod serves the request. Every `app.conf` setting can be passed as an environment variable, so the endpoint can come from a Secret:\\n\\n```yaml\\nreplicaCount: 3\\n\\nenvFromSecret:\\n  - name: redisEndpoint\\n    secretName: casdoor-redis\\n    key: endpoint # host:port, or host:port,db,password\\n```\\n\\nThe same Redis is used for the OAuth device flow state. Once Redis is in place, `autoscaling.enabled: true` adds a HorizontalPodAutoscaler.\\n\\n## Configuration as code\\n\\nClicking organizations and applications together in the web UI is fine for a first test, but most teams want the setup of their identity provider reviewed and versioned like the rest of their infrastructure. The `initData` values do that:\\n\\n```yaml\\ninitData:\\n  enabled: true\\n  data:\\n    organizations:\\n      - owner: admin\\n        name: acme\\n        displayName: Acme\\n        passwordType: bcrypt\\n    applications:\\n      - owner: admin\\n        name: app-acme-portal\\n        organization: acme\\n        displayName: Acme Portal\\n        redirectUris:\\n          - https://portal.acme.example.com/callback\\n    users:\\n      - owner: acme\\n        name: alice\\n        displayName: Alice\\n        password: change-me\\n        signupApplication: app-acme-portal\\n    roles:\\n      - owner: acme\\n        name: admins\\n        displayName: Admins\\n        users: [acme/alice]\\n        isEnabled: true\\n```\\n\\nThe chart stores the data in a Secret and mounts it into the pods. Casdoor applies it at startup, then checks it every 30 seconds (`initData.watchInterval`). When you change the data and run `helm upgrade`, kubelet updates the mounted Secret and the running pods apply it without a restart.\\n\\nA few rules make this safe to use alongside the web UI:\\n\\n- **Only the fields you write are managed.** An object that already exists gets the fields set in the file; its other fields keep the values edited in the UI. You can manage just the redirect URIs of an application, and leave the rest to the admin console.\\n- **Passwords are initial passwords.** A user\'s `password` is only used when the user is created, so users can change theirs afterwards.\\n- **Nothing is deleted.** Removing an object from the file doesn\'t remove it from Casdoor.\\n- **Errors don\'t take Casdoor down.** If an object fails to apply, the error is logged and Casdoor keeps serving; the data is retried on every check until it applies.\\n\\nThe file format is the one Casdoor has always used for [data initialization](/docs/deployment/data-initialization): organizations, applications, users, providers, certs, roles, permissions, groups, LDAP servers, webhooks, plans, pricings and more, with the same field names as the REST API. A quick way to get started is to export an existing instance with `casdoor -export` and keep the objects you want to manage.\\n\\nTo keep the data out of the values file, for example when it is managed by Sealed Secrets or External Secrets, create the Secret yourself:\\n\\n```yaml\\ninitData:\\n  enabled: true\\n  existingSecret: casdoor-init-data\\n  existingSecretKey: init_data.yaml\\n```\\n\\nThe same feature works without Kubernetes. Mount a JSON or YAML file and set:\\n\\n```ini\\ninitDataFile = /init-data/init_data.yaml\\ninitDataMerge = true\\ninitDataWatchInterval = 30\\n```\\n\\n## Or use Terraform\\n\\nIf you prefer `terraform plan`, imports of existing objects and drift detection, the official [Terraform provider](https://registry.terraform.io/providers/casdoor/casdoor) manages the same objects through the Casdoor API:\\n\\n```terraform\\nresource \\"casdoor_application\\" \\"portal\\" {\\n  name          = \\"app-acme-portal\\"\\n  organization  = \\"acme\\"\\n  redirect_uris = [\\"https://portal.acme.example.com/callback\\"]\\n}\\n```\\n\\nUse the Helm values when the configuration should ship with the deployment, and Terraform when Casdoor is one of many systems you already manage with it. See the [Terraform guide](/docs/deployment/terraform).\\n\\n## Coming from the old chart\\n\\nUp to v4.15.0, the chart was published as `oci://registry-1.docker.io/casbin/casdoor-helm-charts`, and the separate `casdoor/casdoor-helm` repository is now archived. The Docker Hub chart still receives every release, but new installs should use the GHCR one. Moving an existing release over is a regular upgrade, and resource names stay the same:\\n\\n```shell\\nhelm upgrade casdoor oci://ghcr.io/casdoor/helm-charts/casdoor --version <version>\\n```\\n\\n## Links\\n\\n- Helm chart: [Artifact Hub](https://artifacthub.io/packages/helm/casdoor/casdoor) \xb7 [source](https://github.com/casdoor/casdoor/tree/master/manifests/casdoor)\\n- Guides: [Try with Helm](/docs/basic/try-with-helm) \xb7 [Data initialization and configuration as code](/docs/deployment/data-initialization#configuration-as-code) \xb7 [Terraform](/docs/deployment/terraform)\\n- Don\'t want to run it yourself? [Casdoor Cloud](https://www.casdoor.com/pricing?utm_source=casdoor.ai&utm_medium=blog&utm_content=kubernetes) gives you a dedicated Casdoor instance that we host and keep upgraded for you."},{"id":"casdoor-joins-cncf-landscape","metadata":{"permalink":"/de/blog/casdoor-joins-cncf-landscape","editUrl":"https://github.com/casdoor/casdoor-website/edit/master/i18n/de/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/2026-02-22-casdoor-joins-cncf-landscape.mdx","source":"@site/i18n/de/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/2026-02-22-casdoor-joins-cncf-landscape.mdx","title":"Casdoor Joins CNCF Cloud Native Landscape","description":"On February 22, 2026, Casdoor joined the CNCF Cloud Native Landscape, under the Security & Compliance category in Provisioning. The Landscape brings together widely used cloud-native projects and products; inclusion helps users and enterprises discover and evaluate solutions for building cloud-native systems.","date":"2026-02-22T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"Casdoor","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/casdoor"},{"inline":true,"label":"CNCF","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/cncf"},{"inline":true,"label":"Cloud Native","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/cloud-native"},{"inline":true,"label":"Landscape","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/landscape"},{"inline":true,"label":"IAM","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/iam"},{"inline":true,"label":"SSO","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/sso"},{"inline":true,"label":"Security","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/security"}],"readingTime":1.94,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Yang Luo","title":"Casdoor Maintainer","url":"https://github.com/hsluoyz","imageURL":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/hsluoyz","key":"hsluoyz","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"casdoor-joins-cncf-landscape","title":"Casdoor Joins CNCF Cloud Native Landscape","authors":["hsluoyz"],"tags":["Casdoor","CNCF","Cloud Native","Landscape","IAM","SSO","Security"],"date":"2026-02-22T00:00:00.000Z"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Running Casdoor on Kubernetes: Official Helm Chart and Configuration as Code","permalink":"/de/blog/casdoor-on-kubernetes"},"nextItem":{"title":"Verbesserung der App-Sicherheit und Benutzererfahrung mit Casdoor Face Authentifizierung SSO","permalink":"/de/blog/face-authentication-casdoor-sso"}},"content":"On February 22, 2026, **Casdoor** joined the [CNCF Cloud Native Landscape](https://landscape.cncf.io/), under the **Security & Compliance** category in Provisioning. The Landscape brings together widely used cloud-native projects and products; inclusion helps users and enterprises discover and evaluate solutions for building cloud-native systems.\\n\\n[https://landscape.cncf.io/?item=provisioning--security-compliance--casdoor](https://landscape.cncf.io/?item=provisioning--security-compliance--casdoor)\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n<img src=\\"/img/blog/cncf-landscape-casdoor.png\\" alt=\\"Casdoor on CNCF Landscape - Security and Compliance section\\" style={{maxWidth: \'100%\', height: \'auto\', border: \'1px solid #eee\', borderRadius: \'8px\'}} />\\n\\n_Casdoor on the CNCF Cloud Native Landscape (Security & Compliance)_\\n\\n## \xdcber Casdoor\\n\\n<div style={{display: \'flex\', alignItems: \'center\', gap: \'1rem\', flexWrap: \'wrap\', marginBottom: \'1rem\'}}>\\n\\n<img src=\\"https://cdn.casbin.org/img/casdoor-logo_1185x256.png\\" alt=\\"Casdoor\\" style={{height: \'80px\', width: \'auto\'}} />\\n\\n</div>\\n\\nCasdoor is an open-source, UI-first Identity and Access Management (IAM) / Single Sign-On (SSO) platform with a web UI. It supports OAuth 2.0, OIDC, SAML, and CAS, as well as many social logins (Google, GitHub, WeChat, etc.), and helps you build user management and SSO in a few steps. Casdoor is developed by [Casbin](https://casbin.org) in Golang and React. It uses a front-end/back-end separation architecture, supports high concurrency, and is actively maintained by the community. The project has received over 13k GitHub stars.\\n\\n## About CNCF and the Cloud Native Landscape\\n\\n<div style={{display: \'flex\', alignItems: \'center\', gap: \'1.5rem\', flexWrap: \'wrap\', marginBottom: \'1rem\'}}>\\n\\n<img src=\\"https://www.cncf.io/wp-content/uploads/2023/04/cncf-main-site-logo.svg\\" alt=\\"CNCF - Cloud Native Computing Foundation\\" style={{height: \'80px\', width: \'auto\'}} />\\n<img src=\\"https://landscape.cncf.io/images/logo_header.svg\\" alt=\\"CNCF Cloud Native Landscape\\" style={{height: \'80px\', width: \'auto\'}} />\\n\\n</div>\\n\\nThe **Cloud Native Computing Foundation (CNCF)** is a Linux Foundation project that promotes cloud-native technologies. Its vision is to make cloud native ubiquitous. CNCF maintains the **Cloud Native Landscape** ([https://landscape.cncf.io](https://landscape.cncf.io)), which classifies and tracks projects and products in the cloud-native ecosystem and serves as a reference map for developers and enterprises.\\n\\n## Looking ahead\\n\\nJoining the CNCF Landscape reflects progress in Casdoor\u2019s maturity and community. We will keep improving Casdoor and its integration with cloud-native workflows, and we welcome more users and contributors to join the community.\\n\\nVerwandte Links:\\n\\n- [Casdoor](https://casdoor.org) \xb7 [GitHub](https://github.com/casdoor/casdoor) \xb7 [Documentation](https://casdoor.org/docs) \xb7 [CNCF Landscape \u2013 Casdoor](https://landscape.cncf.io/?selected=casdoor)"},{"id":"face-authentication-casdoor-sso","metadata":{"permalink":"/de/blog/face-authentication-casdoor-sso","editUrl":"https://github.com/casdoor/casdoor-website/edit/master/i18n/de/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/2023-12-12-face-authentication-casdoor-sso.mdx","source":"@site/i18n/de/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/2023-12-12-face-authentication-casdoor-sso.mdx","title":"Verbesserung der App-Sicherheit und Benutzererfahrung mit Casdoor Face Authentifizierung SSO","description":"In einer Zeit, in der digitales Identit\xe4tsmanagement von entscheidender Bedeutung ist, revolutionieren Single Sign-On (SSO) L\xf6sungen wie Casdoor die Interaktion zwischen Nutzern und verschiedenen Anwendungen. Das SSO von Casdoor vereinfacht den Login-Prozess, indem es Benutzern erm\xf6glicht, sich nur einmal zu authentifizieren, um auf mehrere Dienste zuzugreifen. Dies erh\xf6ht nicht nur die Benutzerfreundlichkeit, sondern erh\xf6ht auch das Engagement und die Zufriedenheit.","date":"2023-12-12T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"Casdoor","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/casdoor"},{"inline":true,"label":"SSO","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/sso"},{"inline":true,"label":"Single-Sign-On","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/single-sign-on"},{"inline":true,"label":"Face Recogniztion","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/face-recogniztion"},{"inline":true,"label":"FaceID","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/face-id"},{"inline":true,"label":"Face","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/face"},{"inline":true,"label":"Machine Learning","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/machine-learning"}],"readingTime":1.76,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"James Lawson","title":"Produktmanager","url":"https://casdoor.org","imageURL":"https://cdn.casbin.com/casdoor/static/admin.png","key":"James Lawson","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"face-authentication-casdoor-sso","title":"Verbesserung der App-Sicherheit und Benutzererfahrung mit Casdoor Face Authentifizierung SSO","authors":["James Lawson"],"tags":["Casdoor","SSO","Single-Sign-On","Face Recogniztion","FaceID","Face","Machine Learning"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Casdoor Joins CNCF Cloud Native Landscape","permalink":"/de/blog/casdoor-joins-cncf-landscape"},"nextItem":{"title":"Willkommen","permalink":"/de/blog/welcome"}},"content":"In einer Zeit, in der digitales Identit\xe4tsmanagement von entscheidender Bedeutung ist, revolutionieren Single Sign-On (SSO) L\xf6sungen wie Casdoor die Interaktion zwischen Nutzern und verschiedenen Anwendungen. Das SSO von Casdoor vereinfacht den Login-Prozess, indem es Benutzern erm\xf6glicht, sich nur einmal zu authentifizieren, um auf mehrere Dienste zuzugreifen. Dies erh\xf6ht nicht nur die Benutzerfreundlichkeit, sondern erh\xf6ht auch das Engagement und die Zufriedenheit.\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\nF\xfcr Unternehmen optimiert das SSO die Verwaltung von Benutzeranmeldungen, erh\xf6ht die Sicherheit und vereinfacht neue Benutzer an Bord. Durch die Zentralisierung der Benutzerverwaltung kann das Risiko mit schwachen oder recycelten Passw\xf6rtern gedrosselt werden.\\n\\nAngesichts wachsender Online-Bedrohungen ist jedoch die Integration robuster Sicherheitsma\xdfnahmen in SSO-Systemen wie Casdoor unerl\xe4sslich. Hier kommt die Gesichtauthentifizierungstechnologie ins Spiel, indem sie eine zus\xe4tzliche Schutzschicht bei der Aufrechterhaltung benutzerfreundlicher Interaktionen hinzuf\xfcgt.\\n\\nFace Authentifizierung verwendet einzigartige Gesichtsmerkmale, um Benutzeridentit\xe4ten zu best\xe4tigen. Es vergleicht Live-Bilder mit gespeicherten Gesichtsvorlagen und bietet eine sichere und unkomplizierte M\xf6glichkeit, Benutzer zu authentifizieren. Diese Methode zeichnet sich durch hohe Genauigkeit auch unter unterschiedlichen Bedingungen aus und wird in modernen Endger\xe4ten weit verbreitet.\\n\\nDie Integration der Gesichtserkennung in Casdoor SSO-System verspricht eine sichere, effiziente und nahtlose Benutzererfahrung. Um eine optimale Leistung zu gew\xe4hrleisten, ist es von entscheidender Bedeutung, sich auf folgende Bereiche zu konzentrieren:\\n\\n1. _Anti-Spoofing Ma\xdfnahmen_: Die Umsetzung der Lebenserkennung, wie das Aufsp\xfcren von Augenbewegungen oder zuf\xe4lligen Gesten, ist unerl\xe4sslich, um den Fehlversuchen entgegenzuwirken und die Systemintegrit\xe4t zu wahren.\\n\\n2. _Regelm\xe4\xdfige Updates zur Verbesserung der Genauigkeit_: Die Aktualisierung der Gesicht-Authentifizierungstechnologie mit den neuesten KI und biometrischen Fortschritten gew\xe4hrleistet Genauigkeit und verbessert das Nutzererlebnis. Es ist wichtig, verschiedene Datens\xe4tze f\xfcr Algorithmustraining zu verwenden, um Vorurteile zu vermeiden.\\n\\n3. _Ausgewogenheit von Sicherheit und Bequemlichkeit_: Die besten Systeme bieten eine perfekte Balance zwischen strengen Sicherheitsma\xdfnahmen und Benutzerfreundlichkeit. Dieser Ansatz ermutigt die Benutzer zu adoptieren, indem er einen einfachen Authentifizierungsprozess sicherstellt, ohne die Sicherheit zu beeintr\xe4chtigen.\\n\\nZusammenfassend l\xe4sst sich sagen, dass Unternehmen durch die Integration der Gesichtserkennung in das SSO von Casdoor ein sicheres, effizientes und benutzerfreundliches Anmeldeerlebnis anbieten k\xf6nnen. Dies st\xe4rkt nicht nur die Sicherheit, sondern passt sich auch den Erwartungen der modernen Nutzer in der digitalen Welt an."},{"id":"welcome","metadata":{"permalink":"/de/blog/welcome","editUrl":"https://github.com/casdoor/casdoor-website/edit/master/i18n/de/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/2021-08-24-welcome.mdx","source":"@site/i18n/de/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/2021-08-24-welcome.mdx","title":"Willkommen","description":"Willkommen auf der offiziellen Casdoor Webseite!\ud83d\udc4b","date":"2021-08-24T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"Casdoor","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/casdoor"},{"inline":true,"label":"SSO","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/sso"},{"inline":true,"label":"Authentication","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/authentication"},{"inline":true,"label":"OAuth2.0","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/o-auth-2-0"},{"inline":true,"label":"Casbin","permalink":"/de/blog/tags/casbin"}],"readingTime":1.44,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"ErikQQY","title":"Casbin Mitglied","url":"https://github.com/ErikQQY","imageURL":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/52615090?s=120&v=4","key":"ErikQQY","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"welcome","title":"Willkommen","authors":["ErikQQY"],"tags":["Casdoor","SSO","Authentication","OAuth2.0","Casbin"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Verbesserung der App-Sicherheit und Benutzererfahrung mit Casdoor Face Authentifizierung SSO","permalink":"/de/blog/face-authentication-casdoor-sso"}},"content":"Willkommen auf der offiziellen Casdoor Webseite!\ud83d\udc4b\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n## Was ist Casdoor?\\n\\nCasdoor is a UI-first [Identity Access Management (IAM)](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Identity_management) / [Single-Sign-On (SSO)](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Single_sign-on) platform based on OAuth 2.0, OIDC, SAML and CAS.\\n\\nCasdoor is developed by [Casbin](https://casbin.org) using Golang and React.\\n\\nZiel von Casdoor ist es, eine leistungsstarke Authentifizierungsplattform f\xfcr die Benutzerverwaltung bereitzustellen.\\n\\n## Warum Casdoor?\\n\\n- \u26a1 Leistungsstarke Funktion und einfache Konfiguration. With support for  <img src=\\"https://cdn.casbin.org/img/social_google.png\\" width=\\"30\\"></img> <img src=\\"https://cdn.casbin.org/img/social_github.png\\" width=\\"30\\"></img> <img src=\\"https://cdn.casbin.org/img/social_facebook.png\\" width=\\"30\\"></img> <img src=\\"https://cdn.casbin.org/img/social_twitter.png\\" width=\\"30\\"></img> <img src=\\"https://cdn.casbin.org/img/social_linkedin.png\\" width=\\"30\\"></img> <img src=\\"https://cdn.casbin.org/img/social_weibo.png\\" width=\\"30\\"></img> <img src=\\"https://cdn.casbin.org/img/social_wechat.png\\" width=\\"30\\"></img> <img src=\\"https://cdn.casbin.org/img/social_qq.png\\" width=\\"30\\"></img> <img src=\\"https://cdn.casbin.org/img/social_dingtalk.png\\" width=\\"30\\"></img>   <img src=\\"https://cdn.casbin.org/img/social_gitee.png\\" width=\\"30\\"></img>  <img src=\\"https://cdn.casbin.org/img/social_wecom.png\\" width=\\"30\\"></img><img src=\\"https://cdn.casbin.org/img/social_gitlab.png\\" width=\\"30\\"></img> sign in and traditional username/password sign in, Casdoor helps you implement your users management system just in a few steps.\\n\\n- \ud83d\udca5 Front-End-Back-End-Trennarchitektur. Casdoor bietet eine man\xf6vrierf\xe4hige Web-Benutzeroberfl\xe4che und unterst\xfctzt eine hohe Parallelit\xe4t.\\n\\n- \ud83e\udd42 Aktive Open Source Community. Casdoor ist Open Source und wird aktiv gepflegt.\\n\\n- \ud83c\udf10 Mehrsprachige Unterst\xfctzung. Casdoor unterst\xfctzt mehrere Sprachen, darunter Deutsch, Chinesisch, Englisch, Franz\xf6sisch und viele mehr.\\n\\n## Wie benutze ich Casdoor?\\n\\nYou can follow the detailed document to learn about [Casdoor API](https://door.casdoor.com/swagger/) and [setup your own Casdoor](/docs/basic/server-installation). Or use the [Casdoor SDKs](/docs/how-to-connect/sdk) to implement your own application."}]}}')}}]);